      "lastReply": "A young tiger named Raja lived deep in the forest, where he spent his days hunting and napping under the banyan trees. One evening, the wind carried the smell of biryani from a nearby village, and Raja's mouth watered. He crept closer and watched through a window as a grandmother layered rice, spices, and tender meat in a heavy pot. That night, he couldn't stop thinking about it.\n\nThe next morning, Raja decided he would learn to cook. He borrowed a pot from an abandoned hut, gathered rice and wild herbs, and tried to remember what he'd seen. His first attempt was a disaster—burnt rice, too much chilli, and no flavour at all. But Raja didn't give up. He watched the villagers again, practised every day, and slowly got better. He learned to fry onions until golden, to layer the rice just right, and to let the pot steam on low heat.\n\nMonths later, Raja invited all the animals of the forest to a feast. When he lifted the lid, the smell of perfect biryani filled the clearing. The deer, the monkeys, even the grumpy old bear couldn't believe a tiger had made it. Raja grinned, his whiskers twitching with pride. \"Cooking,\" he said, \"is just another kind of hunt—you need patience, timing, and a little bit of heart.\" And from that day on, every full moon, the forest gathered for Raja's biryani night.\n\n7:47 AM",
